<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Samora Machel</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1933">1933</a>–<a href="/years/1986">1986</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Samora Machel was a Mozambican revolutionary who commanded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frelimo">FRELIMO</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> army and became its leader after Eduardo Mondlane's assassination in 1969. He was the first President of independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozambique">Mozambique</a> from 1975, building a one-party socialist state while supporting liberation movements in Rhodesia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>. He died in 1986 when his aircraft crashed inside South Africa.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/samora-machel.jpg" alt="Samora Machel" width="320" height="422" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Department of Defense. American Forces Information Service. Defense Visual Information Center. 1994 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Samora_Moises_Machel_detail_DF-SC-88-01383.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Samora Moisés Machel was born on 29 September 1933 in the village of Madragoa, now Chilembene, in Gaza Province, to a family of farmers. Under Portuguese rule his father was classified as an &quot;indígena&quot;, a native, and had to accept lower prices than white farmers, grow labour-intensive <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> and refrain from branding his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>; even so he owned four ploughs and 400 head of cattle by 1940. Machel attended mission schools run by Catholic missionaries, finishing the fourth grade, and trained as a nurse.</p><p>He began his political activity at the Miguel Bombarda hospital in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maputo">Lourenço Marques</a>, protesting that black nurses were paid less than white ones. Warned that the Portuguese political police were watching him, he left the country and made his way to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dar-es-salaam">Dar es Salaam</a> to join <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frelimo">FRELIMO</a>, travelling through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eswatini">Swaziland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/botswana">Botswana</a>.</p><p>He was one of the second group of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frelimo">FRELIMO</a> guerrillas sent for training in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> and then ran the movement's camp at Kongwa. When FRELIMO began the war of independence on 25 September 1964 he became a key commander, notably in Niassa, and became head of its army after the death of Filipe Samuel Magaia in October 1966.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>After the change of government in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a>, Machel rejected General António de Spínola's plan for a referendum on independence, saying that one does not ask a slave if he wants to be free. Talks in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lusaka">Lusaka</a> in June 1974 produced nothing, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frelimo">FRELIMO</a> turned to the military movement then holding power in Portugal. Machel became the first President of independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozambique">Mozambique</a> in 1975.</p><p>He supported nationalist movements in Rhodesia, whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrillas</a> operated from FRELIMO-held areas in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tete">Tete</a> province, and sent Mozambican units to fight alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwe-african-national-union">ZANU</a> guerrillas. In 1977 RENAMO, backed first by Rhodesia and later by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, began a rebellion that plunged the country into civil war. On 30 June 1981 South African commandos attacked houses in Matola, killing 12 members of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-national-congress">African National Congress</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 19 October 1986 Machel attended a summit at Mbala in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambia">Zambia</a>. Although invited to stay overnight, he insisted on returning to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maputo">Maputo</a>, and his plane crashed that night into a hillside at Mbuzini, just inside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>. Machel and 33 others died; nine passengers at the back survived. The Margo Commission concluded that pilot error caused the crash, but the Mozambican and Soviet governments rejected its report, and the Soviet minority report suggested the aircraft was lured off course by a decoy beacon. Speculation continues.</p><p>His state funeral was held in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maputo">Maputo</a> on 28 October 1986, and he was buried in a star-shaped crypt at Mozambican Heroes' Square. Streets in many countries bear his name, and a memorial at the crash site was inaugurated on 19 January 1999 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nelson-mandela">Nelson Mandela</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Samora Machel” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under President <b>Samora Machel</b>, FRELIMO built a one-party state on Marxist principles with support from Cuba and the Soviet Union.</blockquote><a href="/state/mozambique">Mozambique</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Carnation Revolution in Lisbon in 1974, Portugal negotiated withdrawal, and Mozambique became independent on 25 June 1975 under <b>Samora Machel</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/portuguese-mozambique">Portuguese Mozambique</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Gaza Province, in what is now <a href="/countries/mozambique">Mozambique</a>. Died in Mbuzini, in what is now <a href="/countries/south-africa">South Africa</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-23.75,32.75&amp;year=1967" aria-label="Gaza Province on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5423 3039 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Samora Machel"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5873 3309h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5873 3309h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-23.75,32.75&amp;year=1967">What was here in 1967? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
